  (:adr/body "## What this session did

Rotated a dead OpenRouter key that lived in three places, found that
`api.murakumo.cloud`'s relay held a fourth copy of the same dead key, and then
built the credit rail the relay had been missing — merged it, deployed it,
measured it against production, found it refused the operator's own bots, and
rolled both production and `main` back inside the hour.

## The keys — three dead copies, and a fourth in a Worker

`sk-or-v1-d5c…` (live, `is_free_tier: false`) replaced:

| where | old | status |
|---|---|---|
| login Keychain `gftd.openrouter` (hermes-hyakka-bots reads this) | `sk-or-v1-baa…` | 401 `User not found` |
| login Keychain `openrouter` (`~/.zshenv` exports this as `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`) | `sk-or-v1-e78…` | 401 `User not found` |
| `local-murakumo` Worker secret `MURAKUMO_OPENROUTER_API_KEY` | (a fourth) | 401, passed through as HTTP 400 |

The two Keychain items are DIFFERENT items — `gftd.openrouter` (acct
`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`) and `openrouter` (acct `$USER`). That is the whole drift:
`.zshenv` already read from a Keychain, just not the one the workspace policy
(`scripts/hermes-hyakka-bots/free-model-policy.edn`) names as the rotation point.

`api.murakumo.cloud` was diagnosed by discrimination, not by reading the ADR:
`murakumo-main` (fleet-local) returned 200 while `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` and
`qwen/qwen3.8-flash` (relayed) returned 400 carrying the upstream body
`{\\\"error\\\":{\\\"message\\\":\\\"User not found.\\\",\\\"code\\\":401}}` — verbatim
OpenRouter, which is why it read as a murakumo account problem.

## hermes

`~/.hermes/config.yaml`: `model.provider: openrouter`, `model.default:
qwen/qwen3.8-flash`, a `providers.openrouter` block with
`stale_timeout_seconds: 600`, and `secrets.command` reading the Keychain so the
key is never written to disk. `auth.json` `active_provider` `nous` -> `openrouter`.
Gateway restarted 09:53 JST (config mtime 09:45) so it re-ran the secrets helper.

Measured, not assumed: the first verification returned the right answer while
being served by `claude-sonnet-5` on a local claude-code bridge. `--usage-file`
is what showed it. Ten runs, then eight with the fallback chain cleared, isolated
the cause: `qwen/qwen3.8-flash` has exactly ONE upstream on OpenRouter (Alibaba,
confirmed via `/endpoints`) on a shared pool that returns 429 intermittently.
The fallback was rebuilt to leave that pool: `qwen/qwen3.8-flash` ->
`z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` (Z.ai, 1.31M ctx, cheaper) -> the local bridge, last.
Re-measured 10 runs: 9 qwen, 1 glm, 0 bridge.

The local bridge was also broken independently — it answered
`No such tool available` for every tool, which is why it was put last.
FIXED 2026-08-31 (adr-2608311900): the guest reached for the caller's tools
even though the empty --tools flag leaves it holding none, and on a bad run
wrote the CLI's refusal into `content` with `tool_calls` empty — so the caller
was told its own tools were broken. The bridge now names that error in its
directive and salvages the rejected attempt into `tool_calls`, with a test
that fails when the salvage is removed. Ordering it last is still right for
other reasons (it is the slowest lane), but no longer for this one.

## The credit rail — what landed and what it cost

`5e351da` (merged), reverted by `9101dd1b`, preserved as PR #190 (draft).

1. **Meter.** `upstream-cost-micros` reads OpenRouter's own `usage.cost`.
   Nothing is estimated. Absent is `nil`, never `0` — `0` would sort among
   requests that genuinely cost nothing. `relay-meter` rides on
   `:run/spend-for` rather than becoming a new event kind, because
   `credits-admission/event-keys` is a whitelist that drops unknown kinds
   silently.
2. **Charge.** Job id is the gateway's generation id, not `chat-<wall clock>`
   (not idempotent, collides between concurrent requests in the same
   millisecond). `debit-chat-credits!` returned `nil` for success, empty
   wallet, unpriced model, unmetered response and failed append alike.
3. **Reserve.** Affordability decided BEFORE the upstream call, from the same
   price table the settle uses. A **check, not a hold**: `credits/refund` is a
   free constructor whose own docstring calls an ungoverned caller a mint path,
   and `refundable` refunds the whole spend once, keyed by job — it cannot
   express `give back the tokens they did not use`. Concurrent requests can
   still overdraw by the sum of their ceilings; the next is refused. Bounded,
   not atomic.

### Why it was reverted

```
z-ai/glm-5.3-flash + Authorization: MURAKUMO_API_TOKEN
-> 402 {\\\"code\\\":\\\"external_model_insufficient_credits\\\",
        \\\"who\\\":\\\"hermes-agent\\\",\\\"cost\\\":0.015,\\\"balance\\\":0}
```

`MURAKUMO_API_TOKEN` does not authenticate as `:shared-token`. It authenticates
as an **mk1 identity with subject `hermes-agent`**, and `debit-credits?` counts
`:mk1` as a billable customer. So the credential ADR-2608302100 named as
\"the OpenRouter bill itself, credits を引かない\" is, at the wire,
indistinguishable from a paying stranger.

Before the change that caller was served and silently not charged — the hole the
change closes. After it, the operator's own bots are refused. Only one of those
stops live traffic, so production went back to `6ef5790f` and `main` went with it:
**main must stay deployable by anyone who has not read this thread.**

## The defect in how this was verified

253 tests / 1351 assertions were green, and each of the three new claims was
broken in turn and went red for the reason broken. **Every one of them is a
pure-function test.** The reserve wiring was never exercised against a real
identity, so `chat-identity` returning `:mk1` for the operator's own token was
first observed in production. This is CLAUDE.md's own 『手元で緑 は fleet で緑
ではない』, demonstrated by the agent writing about it.

Re-landing must add a test that pins the identity a real `MURAKUMO_API_TOKEN`
resolves to.

## Blocking decision (one line, not a rewrite)

- (a) exclude operator-owned mk1 subjects from `debit-credits?` via an env
  allowlist — matches what ADR-2608302100 already decided
- (b) grant `hermes-agent` credits and treat it as a customer
- (c) move the bots to the shared-token rail

## Found while verifying, NOT caused by it

The fleet-hosted path is down. `/ready` reports `available: 0` of `configured: 1`;
`murakumo-main` returns 500 (Cloudflare 1101) 5 times out of 5 on the
rolled-back version, and the same 1101 is in `~/.hermes/logs/agent.log` from
07:23 JST — three hours before the deploy. `/infer/models/murakumo-main`
resolves the alias correctly, so the alias is fine and the backing node is not
answering.

Also unfixed: the Worker maps upstream 401 and 429 to HTTP **400**, which hermes
treats as retryable (3 attempts observed), and passes the upstream error body
through verbatim. Both were reverted along with the rest.

## Resume point

```bash
# 1. decide (a)/(b)/(c) above, then
gh pr view 190 --repo network-awai/cloud-murakumo-api
# 2. the worktree layout that makes the sibling deps and the release build work:
#    <scratch>/orgs/network-awai/cloud-murakumo-api  with orgs/kotoba-lang symlinked
#    and cloud-murakumo checked out at the manifest pin, then
clojure -Sdeps '{:deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"2.28.20\\\"}}}' \\
  -M -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release worker ui
# 3. gzip -n -9, update release/cljs/manifest.json digests, confirm
node scripts/package-release.mjs
```

Still open, unrelated to the code: restart the Hermes desktop app (orphaned from
launchd at `ppid 1`, so `launchctl kickstart` exits 0 without restarting it; its
`serve` child still holds the dead key in its inherited env, and the model picker
will not show an OPENROUTER group until it restarts). OpenRouter balance is
$14.06 and is now shared between hermes and the Worker.")))
